@conference{Chechik20091,
 abstract = {There is a rapidly growing interest in model-based development as a way to increase the level of abstraction and automation in software engineering. The ultimate goal of model-based development is to improve the software process by promoting the use of models as the primary artifacts of development, and to provide computer-supported technologies to transform models into running systems. Software developers create models in a variety of notations to capture their current understanding of the system. These models continually change as the requirements for and understanding of the system evolves. Moreover, it is often necessary to create multiple versions of software models when exploring design options. These versions must also be managed and analyzed, leading to further change. Hence, developers inevitably end up with a large collection of models, which are often likely to be incomplete and inconsistent. Thus, managing models as they evolve is a major challenge. Maintaining coherence across and within models is a major part of this challenge. The models may overlap in their semantic domains, as information about some concepts appears in multiple models. Tools are needed to identify such overlaps, trace information across multiple models, and propagate changes when information represented in multiple models is updated. While detection and removal of inconsistency is important, we also need tools to explore the consistency relationships, and reasoning techniques that are robust even in the presence of inconsistency [2]. Many important inter-model relationships (sometimes called traceability links) are not currently captured in modeling tools, because the developer's intent when creating or editing models is not captured. Hence, a major focus for our work has been in developing new ways to capture, represent, communicate and use such relationships. We envision a broad use of relationships as part of the general process of model management. In [1], we identified a number of model management operations: match (a.k.a. align) for understanding (ontological and semantic) relationships between model elements, merge - for combining partial information contained in individual models, diff - for computing information present in one model but not in the other, slice - for computing relevant portions of models based on an identified criterion. Model management also include, among others, operations for checking consistency within a collection of models and propagating change. In this talk, I discuss our recent work on (semi)-automated explication of relationships between models, and on various aspects of model management: consistency checking, model merging, and change propagation. © 2009 IEEE.},
 author = {Chechik, Marsha},
 doi = {10.1109/MOMPES.2009.5069131},
